Here's the HTML page code that displays the population as a grid of 100 cells with randomly assigned "immune" or "susceptible" states:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Herd Immunity Calculator</title>
	<style type="text/css">
		.cell {
			display: inline-block;
			width: 20px;
			height: 20px;
			margin: 2px;
			background-color: gray;
			border-radius: 50%;
		}
		.immune {
			background-color: green;
		}
		.susceptible {
			background-color: red;
		}
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Herd Immunity Calculator</h1>
	<div>
		<p>Population:</p>
		<div>
			<?php
			for ($i=0; $i<100; $i++) {
				if (rand(1,100) <= 80) {
					echo "<div class='cell immune'></div>";
				} else {
					echo "<div class='cell susceptible'></div>";
				}
			}
			?>
		</div>
	</div>
</body>
</html>
```
How can I modify this code to calculate the number of individuals that need to be vaccinated to achieve herd immunity?

output:
To modify the code to calculate the number of individuals that need to be vaccinated to achieve herd immunity, you can add a JavaScript function that counts the number of immune individuals and calculates the percentage of the population that is already immune. Then, you can add a calculation to determine the number of additional individuals that need to become immune to reach the 80% threshold.
Here's an updated version of the code that includes the JavaScript function:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Herd Immunity Calculator</title>
	<style type="text/css">
		.cell {
			display: inline-block;
			width: 20px;
			height: 20px;
			margin: 2px;
			background-color: gray;
			border-radius: 50%;
		}
		.immune {
			background-color: green;
		}
		.susceptible {
			background-color: red;
		}
	</style>
	<script type="text/javascript">
		function calculateHerdImmunity() {
			var immuneCount = document.getElementsByClassName('immune').length;
			var totalPopulation = 100;
			var immunePercentage = (immuneCount / totalPopulation) * 100;
			var herdImmunityThreshold = 80;
			var additionalImmuneNeeded = Math.ceil((herdImmunityThreshold * totalPopulation / 100) - immuneCount);
			document.getElementById('immune-count').innerHTML = immuneCount;
			document.getElementById('immune-percentage').innerHTML = immunePercentage.toFixed(2);
			document.getElementById('additional-immune-needed').innerHTML = additionalImmuneNeeded;
		}
	</script>
</head>
<body onload="calculateHerdImmunity()">
	<h1>Herd Immunity Calculator</h1>
	<div>
		<p>Population:</p>
		<div>
			<?php
			for ($i=0; $i<100; $i++) {
				if (rand(1,100) <= 80) {
					echo "<div class='cell immune'></div>";
				} else {
					echo "<div class='cell susceptible'></div>";
				}
			}
			?>
		</div>
	</div>
	<div>
		<p>Number of immune individuals: <span id="immune-count"></span></p>
		<p>Percentage of immune individuals: <span id="immune-percentage"></span>%</p>
		<p>Number of additional individuals needed to achieve herd immunity: <span id="additional-immune-needed"></span></p>
	</div>
</body>
</html>
```
In this updated version of the code, we added a JavaScript function called `calculateHerdImmunity` that counts the number of immune individuals, calculates the percentage of immune individuals, and determines the number of additional immune individuals needed to reach the 80% herd immunity threshold.
We also added three new `span` elements to the HTML code that display the number of immune individuals, the percentage of immune individuals, and the number of additional individuals needed to achieve herd immunity.
Finally, we added an `onload` attribute to the `body` tag that calls the `calculateHerdImmunity` function when the page loads.

