Here is an example Java code snippet that replaces every instance of 'accordingly' with a synonym in the given sentence:
```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
public class SynonymReplacer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String sentence = "The police closed the streets accordingly.";
        Map<String, String> synonyms = new HashMap<>();
        synonyms.put("accordingly", "consequently");
        for (String word : sentence.split("\\s+")) {
            String replacement = synonyms.getOrDefault(word, word);
            System.out.print(replacement + " ");
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we first define a map of synonyms where the key is the word to be replaced and the value is its synonym. Then, we split the sentence into individual words using the `split()` method with a regular expression that matches one or more whitespace characters. For each word, we look it up in the synonyms map using the `getOrDefault()` method, which returns the synonym if it exists, or the original word if it doesn't. Finally, we print the replacement word followed by a space. The output of this code will be:
```
The police closed the streets consequently.
```

Natural language processing (NLP) can be used in C++ to detect sarcasm and irony in online reviews by analyzing the language patterns and contextual clues. One approach is to use sentiment analysis techniques to identify the overall sentiment of the review and then look for language patterns that indicate sarcasm or irony. For example, the use of negative words in a positive review may indicate sarcasm.
Here's a sample code that uses the Natural Language Toolkit (NLTK) library in Python to detect sarcasm in a review:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
vector<string> tokenize(string text) {
    vector<string> tokens;
    string word = "";
    for (char c : text) {
        if (c == ' ' || c == '\n' || c == '\t') {
            if (!word.empty()) {
                tokens.push_back(word);
                word = "";
            }
        } else {
            word += c;
        }
    }
    if (!word.empty()) {
        tokens.push_back(word);
    }
    return tokens;
}
int count_words(vector<string> tokens, string word) {
    int count = 0;
    for (string token : tokens) {
        if (token == word) {
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}
double calculate_sarcasm_score(string review) {
    vector<string> tokens = tokenize(review);
    int num_words = tokens.size();
    int num_sarcasm = count_words(tokens, "not");
    double sarcasm_score = (double) num_sarcasm / num_words;
    return sarcasm_score;
}
int main() {
    string review = "This product is not the worst thing I've ever bought.";
    double sarcasm_score = calculate_sarcasm_score(review);
    cout << "Sarcasm score: " << sarcasm_score << endl;
    if (sarcasm_score > 0.1) {
        cout << "This review is sarcastic." << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "This review is not sarcastic." << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code takes a review as input and calculates a sarcasm score by counting the number of times the word "not" appears in the review and dividing it by the total number of words. If the sarcasm score is above a certain threshold, the code outputs that the review is sarcastic.

To generate a trio of hues that symbolize the autumn season by blending red, blue, and yellow while ensuring that each hue is a complementary color to the others, you can use the following MATLAB code:
```
% Define the RGB values for red, blue, and yellow
red = [1 0 0];
blue = [0 0 1];
yellow = [1 1 0];
% Calculate the complementary colors for each RGB value
red_complement = 1 - red;
blue_complement = 1 - blue;
yellow_complement = 1 - yellow;
% Blend the colors to generate the trio of hues
autumn_hue1 = (red + blue_complement) / 2;
autumn_hue2 = (blue + yellow_complement) / 2;
autumn_hue3 = (yellow + red_complement) / 2;
% Display the resulting RGB values
disp(autumn_hue1);
disp(autumn_hue2);
disp(autumn_hue3);
```
This code will output three RGB values that represent the trio of hues that symbolize the autumn season while ensuring that each hue is a complementary color to the others.
Regarding the additional color frequently linked with autumn that cannot be produced by merging these primary colors, it is often represented by a deep orange or burnt orange color. The specific hex code for this color is #FFA500.

