Sentiment analysis can be used to determine the underlying emotions and intentions behind a given text. In the case of "prayed for guidance," sentiment analysis can help determine whether the old man was feeling hopeful, desperate, or uncertain. 
Here's a MATLAB code that can perform sentiment analysis on a given text using the Natural Language Toolkit (NLTK) library:
```matlab
% Load the NLTK library
py.importlib.import_module('nltk');
% Define the text to analyze
text = 'The old man prayed for guidance.';
% Tokenize the text into individual words
tokens = py.nltk.word_tokenize(text);
% Perform part-of-speech tagging on the tokens
pos_tags = py.nltk.pos_tag(tokens);
% Define a list of positive and negative words
pos_words = {'pray', 'guidance', 'hopeful'};
neg_words = {'old', 'man', 'uncertain', 'desperate'};
% Calculate the sentiment score of the text
pos_score = 0;
neg_score = 0;
for i = 1:length(pos_tags)
    word = char(pos_tags{i}(1));
    pos = char(pos_tags{i}(2));

    if ismember(lower(word), pos_words)
        pos_score = pos_score + 1;
    elseif ismember(lower(word), neg_words)
        neg_score = neg_score + 1;
    end
end
% Determine the sentiment of the text
if pos_score > neg_score
    sentiment = 'positive';
elseif neg_score > pos_score
    sentiment = 'negative';
else
    sentiment = 'neutral';
end
% Display the sentiment and sentiment score
disp(['Sentiment: ' sentiment]);
disp(['Sentiment score: ' num2str(pos_score - neg_score)]);
```
This code tokenizes the text into individual words, performs part-of-speech tagging on the tokens, and calculates a sentiment score based on the presence of positive and negative words in the text. The sentiment score is then used to determine whether the sentiment of the text is positive, negative, or neutral. 
